  {
    "episode_id": 89105,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89105.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 43,
    "turn_text": "Oh, well, you know, the short answer is not enough. And we're working on that. You know, I think homeownership is the key to, you know, stabilizing a lot of, you know, the housing and homelessness crisis that we're seeing across the country. And affordable. Housing is just such a key issue. We can't build new housing nearly fast enough to accommodate these growing communities. And once it's built, you tack on the cost of inflation, labor costs, these houses that are being built in new communities farther and farther out from urban areas, individuals who already live in those communities are priced out of the new houses that are being built. Then you talk about the high cost of housing in urban areas as well. I look at the problem of people who are underhoused or experiencing complete homelessness as really a two-way street. It's the cost of housing. But it's also, you know, the affordability aspect, whether individuals have the level of income that they need. So it's on one hand a cost of housing issue, but it's also an affordability issue. And, you know, we can increase wages. we can make sure that the cost of higher education is more affordable. The Biden administration is forgiving a lot of balloon interest payments on student loans for individuals who have just had this runaway interest. So I also see that as a key component. This is good for the economy when individuals are more able to buy a home It sends a real boom to the real estate market. And then healthcare costs as well, doing everything we can to cap the high cost of healthcare, keeping those other expenses down income up so that housing can be more affordable. Because the cost of housing is just one piece of that puzzle. But the affordability aspect on the other end, the income and the other costs related to that income is another huge piece of the puzzle.",
